# TokenEconomizer — local credentials + run environment (TEMPLATE, tracked in git).
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # then fill in the real values; .env is git-ignored
#
# Format: plain `KEY=value`, one per line. A `#` starts a comment ONLY at the start of a
# line -- a trailing `# ...` after a value is part of the VALUE, not a comment. Quote values containing
# spaces. No `$VAR` interpolation and no command substitution — both the Python loader
# (scripts/_env.py) and the shell loader (scripts/launch/_env.sh) treat values literally.
# Loaders NEVER overwrite a variable already exported in your shell, so a one-off
# `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=... bash scripts/launch/...` still wins.

# --- Agent credentials ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each agent bills its own vendor's API directly:
#   Claude Code -> the Anthropic API on ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   Codex       -> the OpenAI API on OPENAI_API_KEY
#   Copilot CLI -> the GitHub Copilot subscription (or Copilot's own BYOK, further down)
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL and ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN are not read: the launchers say so and unset
# them if they are set, because Claude Code would otherwise honour them.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Regional OpenAI endpoint, ONLY for a key issued against one: a key scoped to a region
# 401s on the global endpoint. Leave it unset for a global key. The launchers refuse any
# host that is not OpenAI's own API.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://us.api.openai.com/v1

# --- Models billed by the sealed agents ---------------------------------------------------
# These label the cost basis and are what the harness passes to the CLIs. Keep an A/B's two
# arms on the SAME model — the comparison is meaningless otherwise.
#
# The main sweep runs claude-opus-4-8 and claude-sonnet-5 on both agents, plus 4 required
# arms on claude-opus-5 (docs/RUNNING.md). main_sweep.sh passes --model per lane
# and that WINS over this variable, so for the sweep leave it commented out.
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-4-8
#
# SPELLING. The older Opus has two spellings and the catalogs disagree: the Anthropic API
# serves the DASHED `claude-opus-4-8` and 404s the dotted form; the Copilot CLI's own
# catalog on a subscription lists the DOTTED `claude-opus-4.8`. Write the dashed form
# everywhere: the launchers spell it for the backend (te_model_for_agent in
# scripts/launch/_env.sh, logged as `[env] model id: ... -> ...`), the Copilot probe
# verifies it against the CLI and switches spelling if a vendor rotates it, and
# te_model_tag folds both to one tree tag (opus48) so the two spellings can never fork a
# tree. `claude-opus-5` and `claude-sonnet-5` have one spelling and are untouched.
# Reasoning effort the sealed agent runs at: low|medium|high|xhigh|max. Part of the cost
# basis, so keep BOTH arms of an A/B on the same level.
# COMMENT THIS OUT for the main sweep: its baseline-low/-medium arms set the level from the
# arm name, and an inherited value makes the driver log an override for every cell.
TE_EFFORT_LEVEL=high
# Small/fast model for Claude Code's own background calls. An unserved id is a per-call
# FAILURE, not a fallback, and te_harness forwards this var into the task container, so a
# wrong value reaches the CLI. Leave it unset to keep Claude Code's own default.
# ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5
# Codex model. `gpt-5.6-sol` is served on /v1/responses by the OpenAI API (probed
# 2026-09-09). THE TRAP is the `-codex` ALIASES (gpt-5.1-codex, gpt-5.2-codex, ...):
# /v1/models lists them and /v1/responses 404s them, so pinning one fails once per task.
# Pin a base id.
# Both gpt-5.6-terra and gpt-5.6-sol are evaluated, each in its OWN results tree (the model
# is part of the cost basis). This picks the default for a command that does not pass MODEL=;
# docs/RUNNING.md section 5 loops the arm catalog over both.
CODEX_MODEL=gpt-5.6-sol

# --- GitHub Copilot CLI (AGENT=copilot) -----------------------------------------------------
# The Copilot CLI is its own sealed agent and needs no API key: it authenticates against
# GitHub from ~/.copilot (or a token in COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN / GH_TOKEN / GITHUB_TOKEN).
# Full guide: docs/COPILOT.md.
#
# Copilot serves its own model catalog, so an Anthropic id is NOT valid here. 'auto' lets
# the CLI route; a pinned id fixes the cost basis but must be one your subscription serves
# (`copilot -p ok --model <id>` tells you). Caveat: 'auto' REJECTS a reasoning-effort flag,
# so TE_EFFORT_LEVEL above is recorded as null and not applied unless you pin a model.
COPILOT_MODEL=auto
# headless/unattended alternative to `copilot /login`
# COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN=
#
# BYOK -- Copilot's own built-in "bring your own key": bill your OWN provider instead of the
# Copilot subscription. THE SUBSCRIPTION IS THE DEFAULT; this is the fallback for a host with no subscription, or one whose monthly quota
# is spent (which surfaces as HTTP 402 `quota_exceeded` and a tree of turns=1/eff_tok=0
# rows). COPILOT_PROVIDER_BASE_URL is the ONE switch: set it and the CLI bills that provider
# and does not use GitHub auth at all. Full variable list: `copilot help providers`.
#
# A BYOK row and a subscription row are DIFFERENT COST BASES. The tree name says
# `copilot_*` either way, so run_config.json -> routing.base_url is what tells them apart:
# the provider URL under BYOK, the literal `github-copilot` on a subscription. Do not pool
# the two (docs/COPILOT.md).
# COPILOT_PROVIDER_TYPE=anthropic            # openai (default) | azure | anthropic
# COPILOT_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com
# COPILOT_PROVIDER_API_KEY=                  # same key as ANTHROPIC_API_KEY above
# NOTE: a trailing `# comment` on the same line becomes part of the value -- both loaders
# treat only a WHOLE-LINE `#` as a comment. Uncomment the bare assignment, not these lines.

# --- Data + results roots (see docs/BENCHMARKS.md, te_data/env.sh) ------------------------
# These are site-specific paths with NO built-in default: the SWE-bench harness raises
# at construction when one is missing, instead of resolving to a path that only exists on
# whichever machine wrote the code. DATA is the root holding swebench/swebench_grading.json
# and is the shorthand TE_SWEBENCH_GRADING falls back to.
DATA=
TE_SWEBENCH_REPO=
TE_SWEBENCH_GRADING=
# External tool baselines (tokeneconomizer/baselines/): where the pinned rtk release binary is
# staged host-side, because the sealed task container cannot reach GitHub. Defaults to
# $DATA/scaffolds when DATA is set. Only the rtk-bearing arms read it: rtk, trimech-rtk,
# trimech-rtk-web and codex-trimech-rtk.
TE_SCAFFOLD_CACHE=
TOKENECONOMIZER_SWEBENCH_TASKS=
TOKENECONOMIZER_WEBBENCH_TASKS=
TOKENECONOMIZER_WEBBENCH_PROJECTS=
TOKENECONOMIZER_RESULTS=

# Every other harness knob -- turn caps, timeouts, the probes, the container CPU limit, the
# answer-key override -- is in the config table in docs/HARNESS.md. Nothing there is
# required: each has a default the harness states.

# --- Host resource budget ------------------------------------------------------------------
# TE_SWEBENCH_MAX_CPUS caps ONE task container (default 16) plus the OMP/MKL/OPENBLAS/NUMEXPR/
# LOKY thread caps that must agree with it -- `--cpus` alone does not change what `nproc`
# reports inside, so a parallel test suite would still size its pool to the whole host and
# thrash. 0 = unlimited. Recorded per row in run_config.json -> cpu_limit.
#
# TE_FLEET_CPUS is the one that bounds the HOST: N concurrent lanes are an N x cap ceiling, so
# the per-container value cannot do it. scripts/launch/fleet_budget.sh writes this onto the
# cgroup every rootless-docker container scope shares, which binds lanes that are ALREADY
# running and needs no restart. 0/none = do not enforce. The Codex launcher asserts it.
# TE_SWEBENCH_MAX_CPUS=4
# TE_FLEET_CPUS=16

# --- Harness seam -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `stub` (offline StubRunner, no Docker/LLM) or `module:factory` returning an AgentRunner.
# stub runs NO agent and grades nothing -- its rows are synthetic, for wiring checks only,
# and the launchers now say so loudly at startup. For a real run point this at a harness --
# the repo's own needs no PYTHONPATH, since the driver puts experiments/ and the core on
# sys.path:
#   RUNNER=te_harness.swebench:make_runner      # SWE-bench
#   RUNNER=te_harness.webbench:make_runner      # Web-Bench (all three agents)
RUNNER=stub
